  only the last one is "notifications":

  1. [1mTasks[0m — a data model, create/edit/complete, persistence. There is nothing to notify about without this, and it
     doesn't exist.
  2. [1m"Care about"[0m — what makes a task yours: you created it, you're assigned, you're watching, you commented. This is
     the genuinely interesting part of your sentence and it's usually where these systems go wrong, by defaulting to
     "notify everyone about everything" and training users to ignore the badge.
  3. [1mChange events[0m — which mutations are noteworthy. Status change, yes; someone fixing a typo in a description,
     probably not.
  4. [1mDelivery[0m — in-app inbox, unread badge, email, push. This is the most visible piece and the least interesting one;
     it's plumbing once 1–3 are settled.

  [1mMy recommendation:[0m don't build all four. Build #1 and #2 as one small thing first, with the change log designed in
  from the start, and hold off on delivery until you can see real events flowing. #2 is cheap to change now and
  expensive later — the "which tasks are mine" rule ends up embedded in every query and every UI surface. #4 is the
  reverse: swapping an in-app inbox for email later is a contained change.

  So before I design anything, I need the answer to the structural question.
